# Frontend (Next.js App Router + TypeScript)

## Structure
- Extremely modular, deeply organized feature-based structure, separated by domain (mirrors backend domains).
- Each feature keeps its own `components/ hooks/ forms/ validation/ types/ services/ (data-access) actions/ utils/`.
- Shared UI lives in a design system (shadcn/ui + Tailwind) in a shared layer — never duplicated across features.
- Types and Zod schemas come from `packages/contracts`; forms use React Hook Form + those Zod schemas.
- UI components must not depend on backend implementation details; consume typed domain contracts only.

## Rendering
- Server Components by default; server-side render pages whenever possible.
- Client Components (`"use client"`) only where browser interactivity requires it; keep them small and leaf-level.
- Every route segment has `loading.tsx` and `error.tsx`; `not-found.tsx` where relevant.

## Data Access & Caching
- Centralize data access in server-side data-access functions, Server Actions, or a BFF/API layer.
- Avoid unnecessary client-side API calls.
- Use Next.js cache tags per domain entity; revalidate via `revalidateTag` when backend invalidation events fire.
- Never expose internal service URLs, database connections, credentials, or infrastructure details to the browser. Only intentionally public values use `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`.
- Env vars validated with Zod at startup; API base URLs and settings come from config — never hardcoded.
- Each feature's data-access layer is the only place that knows about its backend module, so a backend extraction into a microservice changes only that layer.

## UX Quality
- Accessible (WCAG 2.1 AA): semantic HTML, keyboard navigation, ARIA where needed.
- i18n-ready: no hardcoded user-facing strings; use a message catalog.
